%[text] **1. Time Response & Model Estimation** (20 marks)
%[text] Consider the control system below where we are using proportinal control $D(s)=K$ of the position of an electric motor $G(s)$, i.e. $y(t)=\\theta(t)$.
%[text] ![](text:image:0ca1)
%[text] a) (5 marks) For a given gain $K\>0$ the system's step response is shown below:
load("dataFinal22.mat")
plot(t_Q1,y_Q1) %[output:49f936ec]
xlabel('Time (s)') %[output:49f936ec]
ylabel('Output') %[output:49f936ec]
title('Unit Step Response') %[output:49f936ec]
%[text] ![](text:image:3f8d)
%[text] Is the system overdamped, critically damped or underdamped? (Uncomment the correct answer below)
% Uncomment the correct answer
%Q1aSystemIsOverDamped=true
%Q1aSystemIsCriticallyDamped=true
Q1aSystemIsUnderDamped=true %[output:1ec4b004]
%[text] What are the settling time, peak time and % overshoot of the system?
% --- Compute Peak Time ---
[peakValue, peakIndex] = max(y_Q1);
Tp_Q1 = t_Q1(peakIndex);

% --- Compute Percent Overshoot ---
finalValue = y_Q1(end);
POS_Q1 = (peakValue - finalValue) / finalValue * 100;

% --- Compute Settling Time (2% criterion) ---
tolerance = 0.02 * finalValue;
lowerBound = finalValue - tolerance;
upperBound = finalValue + tolerance;

% Find the last time the signal leaves the 2% band
idx = find(y_Q1 < lowerBound | y_Q1 > upperBound);

if isempty(idx)
    Ts_Q1 = 0;  % Already settled
else
    Ts_Q1 = t_Q1(idx(end));
end

% --- Compute Rise Time ---
% find the index of the first entry
Tr_Q1 = t_Q1(find(y_Q1 >= 0.9 * finalValue, 1)) - t_Q1(find(y_Q1 >= 0.1 * finalValue, 1)); 

% Display results
Tr_Q1 % rise time %[output:0a70d6a9]
Ts_Q1 % settling time %[output:04b15919]
Tp_Q1 % peak time %[output:85236f55]
POS_Q1 % POS_Q1 / 100 would convert it to fraction %[output:40e892ad]
%[text] b) (5 marks) Based upon your answers to (a) above, for the closer loop system what are the damping ratio $\\zeta$ and natural frequency $\\omega\_n$ of the closed loop system?   
PO = POS_Q1 / 100;                         % convert to fraction
zeta_Q1 = -log(PO) / sqrt(pi^2 + log(PO)^2);
omega_n_Q1 = pi / (Tp_Q1 * sqrt(1 - zeta_Q1^2));

zeta_Q1 %[output:6a962ac2]
omega_n_Q1 %[output:002ee33b]
% --- Closed-loop 2nd-order model: Gcl(s) = K*wn^2 / (s^2 + 2*zeta*wn*s + wn^2)
K_cl      = y_Q1(end);     % DC gain matches steady-state output to unit step
num_Q1    = K_cl * omega_n_Q1^2;
den_Q1    = [1  2*zeta_Q1*omega_n_Q1  omega_n_Q1^2];

Gcl_Q1    = tf(num_Q1, den_Q1);   % estimated closed-loop TF

% Your code goes here
Gcl_Q1 %[output:3c833e6f]

% Uncomment the code below to plot the original data and the step response
% of your approximation

plot(t_Q1,y_Q1) %[output:12d901fa]
hold on %[output:12d901fa]
step(Gcl_Q1) %[output:12d901fa]
hold off %[output:12d901fa]

%[text] c) (5 marks) Given what you know about root locus asymptotes and the general form of a motor's position transfer function from the lab, what are the open loop pole locations of the electric motor plant $G(s)$? (HINT: Think of how you get the asymptotes for the root locus of a typical motor position transfer function with proportional feedback.)
% --- Open-loop 2nd-order model: G(s) = K / (s^2 + 2*zeta*wn*s)
% You code goes here
G_Q1 = tf(K_cl, [1 2*zeta_Q1*omega_n_Q1 0])     %[output:3a557df3]
% Get pole locations
poles_Q1 = pole(G_Q1) %[output:91944fd2]
%[text] d) (5 marks) Suppose we use a PID controller $D(s) = \\frac{K(s+0.5)(s+3)}{s}$  for plant $G(s) = \\frac{20}{s^2 +6s+144}$ in the closed loop control system:
%[text] ![](text:image:3571)
%[text]  Sketch the root locus for $K \> 0$.
% You code goes here
s = tf('s');

% Define plant G(s)
G = 20/(s^2 + 6*s + 144);              % e.g., G = Kp / (s^2 + a*s + b);

K = 1; % For Open-loop transfer function, K = 1
% Define lead/lag controller D(s)
% Example: D(s) = K * (s+z1)/(s+p1) * (s+z2)/(s+p2);
D = K * (s+0.5)*(s+3)/s;

L = D*G;              % open-loop transfer function

figure; %[output:725b6fb2]
rlocus(L);            % root locus of D(s)G(s) %[output:725b6fb2]
grid on; %[output:725b6fb2]

% Optional: overlay constant damping ratio lines
