%[text] Consider the control system:
%[text] ![](text:image:3616)
%[text] in the case when:
%[text] $D(s)=K\\;\\mbox{ and }\\; G(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s+2)(s+4)(s+6)}$
%[text] a) (5 marks) Sketch the root locus for the control system using matlab. 
% Your code goes here
clear
s = tf('s');

% Plant G(s)
G = 1 / (s*(s+2)*(s+4)*(s+6));

% Controller D(s) = K (gain only)
D = 1;

% Open-loop transfer function
L = D * G;

% Plot root locus
figure; %[output:8c94f011]
rlocus(L); %[output:8c94f011]
grid on; %[output:8c94f011]
%[text] b) (5 marks) Assuming in the feedback configuration shown above D(s) = K is chosen so that the closed loop system is stable. 
%[text] What is the type of the system? Uncomment and set variable $\\texttt{n\\\_type}$ to the type of the system.  
% Your code goes here
n_type=1 %[output:7cbc6f17]
%[text] The open‑loop transfer function, L(s)=1/s(s+2)(s+4)(s+6)​, has one pole at the origin.
%[text] A system’s type equals the number of pure integrators (poles at s = 0) in the open‑loop transfer function.
%[text] This means the system will track a step input with zero steady-state error, but will have a finite steady-state error to a ramp input.
%[text] 
%[text] What is its static error constant for a given gain $K$? Put your code below to compute the static error constant and uncomment and  assign it to the variable $\\texttt{K\\\_staticError}$. 
%[text] **NOTE:** I suggest using the $\\texttt{minreal}$matlab command to help you answer this question. Type $\\texttt{help minreal}$at the command prompt to understand how this command works.
% Your code goes here
syms s K
L = K/(s*(s+2)*(s+4)*(s+6));
Kv = limit(s*L, s, 0);
K_staticError = Kv %[output:7b26c631]
%[text] For a given controller gain $K$, what is the steady state error of the closed loop system in response to a unit ramp input? Assign your answer the variable $\\texttt{e\\\_rampQ3b}$
% Your code goes here
e_rampQ3b=1/K_staticError %[output:1230bdd3]
%[text] 
%[text] c) (5 marks) If you want to design a closed loop control system that results in a step response with 15% overshoot and 3 second settling time, assuming ideal 2nd order behaviour, what would be the new location for the dominant poles?
% Your code goes here
% Desired performance
Mp = 0.15;   % 15% overshoot
Ts = 3;        % 3-second settling time 

syms zeta

% Overshoot equation: Mp = exp(-pi*zeta/sqrt(1 - zeta^2))
eq = Mp == exp(-pi*zeta/sqrt(1 - zeta^2));

% Solve for zeta in the valid range (0 < zeta < 1)
zeta_val = double(vpasolve(eq, zeta, [0 1]));

% Natural frequency from Ts = 4/(zeta*wn)
wn = 4/(zeta_val * Ts);

% Dominant poles
sigma = -zeta_val * wn;
wd    = wn * sqrt(1 - zeta_val^2);

% Final answer: dominant pole pair
s_d = [sigma + 1i*wd; sigma - 1i*wd] %[output:5408e28f]
%[text] d) (5 marks) Design a PD compensator $D(s)=K\\left (s+z\_c\\right)$ to place the dominant poles at the location you computed in part (c).
% Your code goes here.

s = tf('s');

% Plant
G = 1 / (s*(s+2)*(s+4)*(s+6));

% Desired dominant pole (from part c)
s_d = -(4/3) + 2.2080i; % Hardcoded

% Define G(s) as function handle for complex evaluation
G_s = @(s) 1./(s.*(s+2).*(s+4).*(s+6));

% Solve for real zero z_c so that angle condition is satisfied
fun = @(z) imag((s_d + z) .* G_s(s_d));   % imag of open-loop at s_d must be zero
z_c = fzero(fun, 0)                      % initial guess 0 %[output:16a92839]

% Now use magnitude condition |L(s_d)| = 1 to find K
L_noK = (s_d + z_c) .* G_s(s_d);
K = 1 / abs(L_noK) %[output:85a0e491]

% Continuous-time PD compensator D(s) = K (s + z_c)
z_d = []                                 % not used here, keep for completeness %[output:1f4a61f7]
D = K * tf([1 z_c], 1) %[output:2e763c87]
