%[text] **4. State Space Control** (20 marks)
%[text] Assume that the plant is given by:
%[text] $G(s) = \\frac{(s+4)(s-4)}{s(s+1)(s+2)(s+5)} = \\frac{(s^2-16)}{s^4+8s^3+17s^2+10s}$
%[text] a) (5 marks) Given G(s) what is the controller canonical (phase variable) state space representation of the plant G(s)?
% Your code goes here
A = [ 0   1   0    0; %[output:group:781a12a0] %[output:5781eacf]
      0   0   1    0; %[output:5781eacf]
      0   0   0    1; %[output:5781eacf]
      0  -10 -17  -8 ] %[output:group:781a12a0] %[output:5781eacf]
B = [0; 0; 0; 1] %[output:535e34c3]
C = [-16 0 1 0] %[output:6a98b7ed]
D = 0 %[output:07e87749]
%[text] b) (5 marks) You want to design state-variable feedback controller that has 20.8% overshoot and settling time of 4 seconds for plant.
%[text] What are the desired locations of the 2 dominant closed loop poles of the system?
% Your code goes here
% Desired specs
PO = 0.208;      % 20.8% overshoot
Ts = 4;         % 4 s settling time 

% Solve for damping ratio zeta from PO formula
zeta = -log(PO) / sqrt(pi^2 + (log(PO))^2);

% Natural frequency from Ts ≈ 4/(zeta*wn)
wn = 4/(zeta*Ts);

% Desired dominant poles
sigma = -zeta*wn;
wd    = wn*sqrt(1 - zeta^2) %[output:11b52268]
p1 = sigma + 1j*wd;
p2 = sigma - 1j*wd;

p1, p2 %[output:51e3b0b3] %[output:2fd19a22]
%[text] **Where would you place the other two poles? Why.**
%[text] Place the remaining two poles significantly farther left on the real axis than the dominant pair, e.g.:
%[text] - s\_3= -5
%[text] - s\_4= -6 \
%[text] Reason:
%[text] Poles far to the left (at least 5 time farther left than the dominant pair) have much faster dynamics (large negative real part), so their modes decay quickly and do not significantly affect the transient response. This keeps the closed‑loop behavior effectively governed by the designed dominant pair at -1 + j\*2.0007 and -1 - j\*2.0007, preserving the desired 20.8% overshoot and 4 s settling time.
%[text] 
%[text] 
%[text] c) (5 marks) Use whatever matlab command(s) you want to compute a state feedback control matrix $\\mathbf{K}$ to place the poles at the desired locations from (b) with the state feedback control $u(t)=-\\mathbf{K}\\mathbf{x}(t)$.
%[text] 
% Your code goes here
%% Choose remaining poles (fast, real, non-dominant)
p3 = -5; % hardcoded based on b's reasoning
p4 = -6; % hardcoded based on b's reasoning

desired_poles = [p1 p2 p3 p4];

%% Check controllability
Co = ctrb(A,B);
rank_Co = rank(Co) %[output:386af734]

if rank_Co == size(A,1) %[output:group:79084f79]
    disp('System is controllable') %[output:98af7fdd]
else
    disp('System is NOT controllable')
end %[output:group:79084f79]

%% Compute state feedback gain K
K = place(A, B, desired_poles) %[output:2c8a1796]
