%[text] **5. Bode Plot Margins, Bandwidth & Digital Control** (20 marks)
%[text] Consider the controller $D(s)=K\\left(s+z\_c\\right)=47.25(s+1.75)$and plant $G(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s+2)(s+4)(s+6)}$ in the closed loop system shown below:
%[text] ![](text:image:9842)
%[text] a) (5 marks) Plot the open loop bode plot for $D(s)G(s)$ using matlab.
% Your code goes here
clear
s = tf('s');

% Define plant and controller
G = 1 / (s*(s+2)*(s+4)*(s+6));
D = 47.25*(s + 1.75);

% Open-loop transfer function
L = D * G;

% a) Bode plot
figure; %[output:7eda1785]
bode(L); %[output:7eda1785]
grid on; %[output:7eda1785]
title('Open-Loop Bode Plot of D(s)G(s)'); %[output:7eda1785]
%[text] What is the gain margin $\\texttt{Gm}$ of the system and what is the phase margin $\\texttt{Pm}$ and at what frequencies do they occur of the system and for the above system? 
% Your code goes here
% Gain and phase margins
[Gm_absolute, Pm, Wcm, Wcp] = margin(L);
% Display results
Gm = 20*log10(Gm_absolute);
Gm  % Gain margin (decibles) %[output:2d956083]

Pm  % Phase margin (degrees) %[output:0528c255]
Wcm % Phase crossover frequency (rad/s) - where gain margin occurs %[output:9d98d5ec]
Wcp % Gain crossover frequency (rad/s) - where phase margin occurs %[output:7b613001]
%[text] 
%[text] b) (5 marks) Currently the controller is $D(s)=K\\left(s+z\_c\\right)=47.25(s+1.75)$, i.e. the gain $K=47.25$. What is the minimum gain $K^\*$ at which the feedback control system above become Bounded Input Bounded Output (BIBO) unstable?
% Your code goes here
% Minimum gain K* at which system becomes BIBO unstable
% System becomes unstable when K increases by the gain margin factor
Kstar = 47.25 * Gm_absolute %[output:2a504664]
%[text] 
%[text] For the current controller $D(s)=K\\left(s+z\_c\\right)=47.25(s+1.75)$, how large a time delay (in seconds) could be introduced into the system control loop before the system would become unstable?
% Your code goes here
% Maximum time delay before instability
% Phase margin tells us how much phase loss is tolerable
% A time delay e^(-Ls) adds phase of -L*Wcp radians at crossover frequency
% Instability occurs when phase loss = Pm
% Pm (in radians) = delay * Wcp
delay = (Pm * pi/180) / Wcp %[output:11b81355]
%[text] c) (5 marks) For the original controller $D(s)=K\\left(s+z\_c\\right)=47.25(s+1.75)$, compute the closed loop transfer function. Find the bandwidth of the closed loop system using Matlab and assign it to the variable $\\texttt{omega\\\_BW}$:
%[text] 
% Your code here
% Closed-loop transfer function
Gcl_Q5 = feedback(L, 1) %[output:2e5701da]

% Find bandwidth of closed-loop system
% bandwidth() returns the -3dB frequency
omega_BW = bandwidth(Gcl_Q5) %[output:5730da94]
%[text] Now suppose that we want to use a digital computer to implement the controller. What is the minimum sampling rate in samples/second (i.e. Hz that you would expect the digital controller system to be stable? 
% Your code here
% Minimum sampling rate (Nyquist criterion)
% Sampling rate must be at least 20x bandwidth for stable digital control
% (rule of thumb: fs >= 20 * omega_BW)
fs_min = 20 * omega_BW / (2*pi)  % convert rad/s to Hz %[output:76fed0b9]
%[text] d) (5 marks) For a sampling rate of 10 Hz compute the complete Zero Order Hold equivalent of $D(s)G(s)$.
% Your code goes here
% d) Zero Order Hold discretization at 10 Hz
Ts = 1/10;  % sampling period = 0.1 seconds

% ZOH equivalent of D(s)G(s)
CG_zoh = c2d(L, Ts, 'zoh') %[output:201206c5]
%[text] What is the closed loop transfer function of the digital system? Is the closed loop digital system stable or unstable? Justify your answer.
% Closed-loop digital transfer function
Gcl_zoh = feedback(CG_zoh, 1) %[output:5c3138d8]

% Check stability - all poles must be inside unit circle
poles_zoh = pole(Gcl_zoh) %[output:8043a1a6]
% Uncomment the correct answer
Gcl_zoh_is_stable = true %[output:817a8e7d]
%Gcl_zoh_is_unstable = true
%[text] All pole magnitudes are less than 1, meaning all poles lie inside the unit circle. Therefore the closed-loop digital system is stable.

